Chapter: ViewYawSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter ViewYaw
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 ViewYaw: the extra camera yaw on top of the bike's heading.

 Two lines of arithmetic that swing the WHOLE view -- the distracted gaze toward
 the pigs plus a subtle head-turn into the lean -- and everything that draws goes
 through one of them. Nothing in judge/ grades the chapter directly; it is
 covered only where a whole frame is compared, which is the most expensive place
 to learn that a coefficient moved.

 WHERE THE EXPECTED VALUES COME FROM: `gaze-yaw + 0.15 * tilt`, evaluated in
 IEEE-754 doubles. Not in decimal -- and that distinction earns its keep on the
 fourth case. 0.15 is not representable in binary, so `0.125 + 0.15 * (-0.75)`
 is 0.012500000000000011 and not 0.0125. The want below carries that value.
 Writing the tidy decimal and reaching for a tolerance to cover the difference
 would have hidden a real property of the chapter behind a fudge; the arithmetic
 is exact, it is just not the arithmetic base ten suggests, so every line here
 is graded at 0.0.

 THE STATES COVER BOTH SIGNS OF BOTH INPUTS, because the fold is a sum and a sum
 is where a sign error survives: a `-` for a `+` on the gaze term is invisible
 whenever the gaze is zero, and the rider spends most of a straight there.

 WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT GRADE, said plainly rather than left to be discovered.
 `pose-for` needs a `List Segment`, and a Segment carries trees, cows, pigs and
 a Cat -- building one here would be more fixture than test, and building the
 real world would put the whole of World under a spec that is about two lines of
 addition. Its only content beyond the fold graded below is
 `(list-at w s.segment).width / 2.0`, and the whole-frame check covers that.

 We say:

Section: The Rider States

 Five states: at rest, a right lean with gaze, a left gaze with a right lean,
 the mixed-sign case, and a pure lean with no gaze at all.

  s0 : RiderState
  s0 = RiderState { segment = 0, along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, v = 0.3, tilt = 0.0, heading = 0.0, gaze-yaw = 0.0, focus = 0.0 }

  s1 : RiderState
  s1 = RiderState { segment = 0, along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, v = 0.3, tilt = 0.125, heading = 4.5, gaze-yaw = 0.375, focus = 0.0 }

  s2 : RiderState
  s2 = RiderState { segment = 0, along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, v = 0.3, tilt = 0.5, heading = 1.0, gaze-yaw = -0.25, focus = 0.0 }

  s3 : RiderState
  s3 = RiderState { segment = 0, along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, v = 0.3, tilt = -0.75, heading = -2.0, gaze-yaw = 0.125, focus = 0.0 }

  s4 : RiderState
  s4 = RiderState { segment = 0, along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, v = 0.3, tilt = 1.25, heading = 0.5, gaze-yaw = 0.0, focus = 0.0 }

Section: The Fold

 head-yaw-frac is 0.15, and it is pinned: it is the only tunable in the chapter
 and the one thing a passing edit would reach for.

  frac-got : List Real
  frac-got = [head-yaw-frac]

  frac-want : List Real
  frac-want = [0.15]

  yaw-got : List Real
  yaw-got = [view-yaw-for s0, view-yaw-for s1, view-yaw-for s2, view-yaw-for s3, view-yaw-for s4]

  yaw-want : List Real
  yaw-want = [0.0, 0.39375, -0.175, 0.012500000000000011, 0.1875]

 heading-for adds the absolute world heading on top, so it is the view yaw again
 plus one term. Grading it separately catches the case where the two lines were
 collapsed into one and the heading dropped -- which would leave the horizon
 still and the scene turning.

  head-got : List Real
  head-got = [heading-for s0, heading-for s1, heading-for s2, heading-for s3, heading-for s4]

  head-want : List Real
  head-want = [0.0, 4.89375, 0.825, -1.9875, 0.6875]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "vy-frac" frac-got frac-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "vy-yaw " yaw-got yaw-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "vy-head" head-got head-want 0.0)
  end
